1. Introduction 
 
Nuclear fusion energy holds the potential to comprehensively address the global energy crisis. Based on the insights gained from the design and manufacturing of the International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER), China proposed the China Fusion Engineering Test Reactor (CFETR) in 2011. This experimental power plant aims to achieve the level of a demonstration reactor (DEMO), with an approximate power output of 1 GW, twice that of ITER [ 1 , 2 ]. The peak magnetic field strength of the CFETR's Central Solenoid (CS) and Toroidal Field (TF) systems have been significantly increased, compared to other Tokamak devices such as ITER and EAST, requiring significant improvements in the strength of the superconducting jacket material to overcome the resultant high electromagnetic force. Notably, conventional materials such as 316LN and JK2LB, used in ITER, fail to satisfy the design requirements. While Japan and Russia have developed IN908 high-temperature alloys and titanium alloys , respectively, such materials were eliminated due to poor welding performance and decreased toughness and plasticity after aging [ [3] , [4] , [5] , [6] ]. Consequently, it becomes imperative to design and develop a new type of ultra-low temperature used and high-strength non-magnetic structural steel for the successful construction of CFETR. 
 
The room temperature strength of N50 austenitic stainless steel is significantly higher than that of 316 stainless steel by introducing high nitrogen content [ 7 ]. Additionally, N50 steel demonstrates superior corrosion resistance and welding performance [ 8 ]. In our previous work, the N50 steel was optimized by suppressing its intergranular sensitization after the Nb3Sn superconducting coil aging thermal treatment, maintaining high low-temperature toughness and plasticity [ 9 , 10 ]. Furthermore, the δ ferrite which is harmful when operating in ultra-high magnetic fields was also eliminated [ 11 ]. However, these works were limited to the laboratory stage, there are still numerous challenges in the industrial manufacturing process of the jacket pipes. For instance, higher nitrogen content represents a higher deformation resistance [ [12] , [13] , [14] ]. Mataya et al. [ 15 ] confirmed that N50 steel has higher hot deformation resistance than other austenitic alloys such as 304 L, 316 L, JBK-75, and ranks only after the IN718 nickel-based alloy [ 14 ]. Hence, the deformation behavior of N50 is supposed to be researched, besides, the recrystallization mechanism which seriously affects its deformation ability is rarely reported, in addition, the whole fabrication process of N50 jacket pipes is also a new challenge. 
 
In this paper, the recrystallization behavior of N50 steel during hot deformation was discussed, and the processing map, prevalent to optimize the hot-workability and control the microstructure of various alloys [ [16] , [17] , [18] , [19] , [20] , [21] , [22] ], was constructed. The optimal hot extrusion parameters were further determined by finite element simulation based on the established constitutive equation (Arrhenius constitutive model) which represents the relationship between flow stresses and working parameters [ 17 , [23] , [24] , [25] , [26] , [27] ]. In addition, the whole process of actual production process of superconducting jackets, such as hot extrusion, cold drawing, compaction, thermal aging, bending, and straightening, was also verified. The N50 steel jacket pipes with excellent performance was successfully fabricated, which provided a theoretical and experimental basis for its industrial manufacturing. 
2. Experiments 
2.1. Materials preparation 
 
Three 200 kg ingots (numbered 1#, 2#, and 3#) with the same compositions were melted by a vacuum melting furnace, and the nominal composition was shown in Table 1 . The ingots were forged into φ217 mm forging billets by three-way upsetting and drawing process, and a small part of 1# billet was obtained by sawing machine to machine φ8 mm × 12 mm hot compression samples. The remaining forgings are used for pipe fabrication. 
 
