2.3. Microstructure characterization 
 
The hot-compressed samples were polished with 2000 # sandpaper and then hot-corroded in 1 g K 2 MnO 4 -10ml H 2 SO 4 -90 ml H 2 O water bath for 10 min to obtain optical microscope (OM) samples. The microstructure after hot deformation was analyzed with electron backscatter diffraction (EBSD, Zeiss supra 55) with a scan step size of 0.15 μm, and the database obtained from EBSD were processed with the software AZtecCrystal. The electron channel contrast imaging (ECCI) technology is used to observe defects such as dislocations inside the grains after deformation. The samples for EBSD and ECCI observation were prepared by the electrolytic polishing method (10 vol % perchloric acid and 90 vol % ethyl alcohol). 
2.4. Fabrication and performance evolution of pipes 
 
The finite element simulation was carried out by DEFORM-3D software to optimize the hot extrusion parameters. The thermal physical parameters such as thermal diffusion coefficient and thermal conductivity used in the simulation were shown in the Supplementary Material Table S1 . A 63 MN horizontal hot extrusion press was used to perform hot extrusion experiments on forgings. The hot extrusion schematic diagram and die diagram are shown in Fig. 2 . Samples from the final pipes for mechanical properties were cut longitudinally by wire cutting. Due to the size limitation, M8 round rod and stretched sheet samples were used in the corner and the face positions of the pipe. The sampling diagram and specific size are shown in Fig. 3 a and b. The 4.2 K tensile test was performed in liquid helium (LHT) environment through SANS CMT5105S with a tensile rate of 1 mm/min according to the GB/T 228.4–2019 standard. The fracture toughness test method at 4.2 K was based on the JIS Z-2284 standard. The fracture toughness samples specifications: W = 25 mm, thickness d = 2.5 mm without side groove, and the sampling diagram and specific size are shown in Fig. 3 a and c. 
