3. Result and discussion 
3.1. Establishment of constitutive relation 
 
Fig. 1 b illustrates the initial microstructure of the forging. The modified N50 steel displays a fully austenitic structure with small recrystallized grains present in some triple junctions. The average grain size is 38 μm. As Fig. 4 depicts, the flow stress behavior of N50 austenitic steel is similar to that of most conventional austenitic steels. The flow stress rapidly increases initially due to the accumulation of dislocations. Subsequently, the rate of stress increase slows down until the peak stress (σ p ) is reached. This is mainly due to the softening effect of dynamic recrystallization (DRX), which counters the hardening effect. After reaching the peak stress, the softening mechanisms (including DRX and dynamic recovery (DRV)) gradually dominate over the hardening effects, resulting in a balance between hardening and softening effects finally. Additionally, as illustrated in Fig. 4 , with the increase in deformation temperature, both peak stress and steady-state stress gradually decrease. However, with the increase in deformation rate, the yield stress significantly increases at all temperatures. The reasons will be discussed in the next chapter. 
