The relationship between stress, strain rate , and temperature in the deformation process of N50 austenitic stainless steel can be described in detail by the constitutive relationship. The Arrhenius-type [ 23 , 28 ] constitutive equation, expressed in Eqs (1) , (2) , (3) , is currently accepted widely. (1) ε ˙ = A 1 σ n 1 for α⋅σ < 0.8 (2) ε ˙ = A 2 exp ( β σ ) for α⋅σ > 1.2 (3) ε ˙ = A [ sinh ( α σ ) ] n exp ( − Q R T ) for all σ. 
 
The constants A, n 1 , n, β, and α in the equation are temperature-independent, where α is equal to β/n1. A1 and A2 are temperature-dependent constants, σ is the flow stress (in MPa), R is the gas constant (R = 8.314 J/mol•K), Q is the deformation activation energy (kJ/mol), and έ is the strain rate (s −1 ). Eq. (1) is used at low stresses when ασ < 0.8, while Eq. (2) is used at high stresses when ασ > 1.2. Eq. (3) , proposed by Sellars and Tegart, is used at all stress levels. Taking the natural logarithm, Eq. (3) can be rewritten as follows: (4) ln ε ˙ = ln A + n ln [ sinh ( α σ ) ] − Q R T 
 
Fig. 5 (a) and (b) present the relationship of lnσ-lnέ and σ-lnέ respectively, and the values of n1 and β determined based on the inverse of the slope of the linear regression lines are 9.780327 and 0.047610096. As a result, the α was calculated as 0.0048679. Furthermore, the value of n 6.832 was obtained according to the inverse slopes in the ln[sinh(ασ)]-lnέ plot depicted in Fig. 5 c. The value of Q, determined from the ln[sinh(ασ)]-10000/T plot illustrated in Fig. 5 d, was 578,706.9 J/mol. This value is higher than that of ordinary AISI 304 N (426 kJ/mol) [ 29 ], 316LN (420.677 kJ/mol) [ 30 ], 316 (413.8 kJ/mol) [ 31 ], 317LN (533 kJ/mol) [ 32 ], and TP 347H (555.1 kJ/mol) [ 33 ] steels, but similar to some Ni-based superalloy (571.5–589.0 kJ/mol) [ 34 ]. This may be attributed to that more nitrogen atoms in N50 steel cause a stronger solute drag effect on dislocation motion, inhibiting the propagation of dislocations and GBs during deformation. It is evident that the activation energy increases with the nitrogen content. For instance, Mn18Cr18 N, which has a higher nitrogen content of 0.61 wt%, exhibits an activation energy of 657.4 kJ/mol [ 35 ]. 
