3.2. Behavior of recrystallization 
3.2.1. Effect of temperature on recrystallization 
 
Fig. 7 indicates that various temperatures result in a significant effect on microstructure. At 950 °C ( Fig. 7 a), the microstructure mainly consists of elongated original grains. From the kernel average misorientation (KAM) image ( Fig. 7 c), it can be seen that there is a high density of dislocations in grains, with only a small number of fine DRX grains distributed along the original high-angle GBs. In addition, a large number of low-angle GBs and sub-grain boundaries are formed inside the grains. The local misorientation of the unrecrystallized region (along the straight line L1 in Fig. 7 a) can exceed 15°, and the accumulated misorientation in the corresponding area also exceeds 25° ( Fig. 7 b). This typical characteristic, where the misorientation gradually increases from sub-grain boundaries to high-angle GBs within the deformed grain, is consistent with CDRX [ 38 , 39 ]. At 1050 °C ( Fig. 7 d), the number and size of DRX grains significantly increase, where forming a grain structure similar to a necklace indicates a typical DDRX characteristic. Sub-grain boundaries with misorientation exceeding 5° are also formed inside the grains ( Fig. 7 e). Under 1150 °C ( Fig. 7 g), the original grain structure has been completely replaced by large recrystallized grains, moreover, the intragranular dislocations and sub-grains remarkably decreases and convert into CDRX grains (white arrows in Fig. 7 g). When deformed at 1250 °C, the DRX grains significantly coarsen, and the number of dislocations inside the grains ( Fig. 7 l) further decrease. 
