Increasing the deformation temperature can significantly improve the kinetics of austenite DDRX. The bowing out of the GBs and the growth of DDRX grains are both the migration process which is activated by the heat input. The GB mobility M and temperature T satisfy the Arrhenius relationship [ [40] , [41] , [42] ]: (8) M = M 0 exp ( − Q R T ) where M 0 is a constant related to the material, Q is the activation energy of GB migration, and R is the gas constant. Eq. (8) indicated that the GB mobility M increases rapidly with the rise of temperature. Therefore, increasing the temperature can significantly increase the nucleation and growth of the DDRX grains, resulting the proportion of the high-angle GB increases rapidly ( Fig. 7 m). However, the number of medium-angle GBs (10–15°) decreases slightly with the increase of temperature, indicating that the increase of temperature is not conducive to CDRX, which is mainly due to the consumption of high-density dislocations and subgrains in the grains by DDRX [ 38 ]. 
3.2.2. Effect of strain rate on recrystallization 
 
The grains with the grain orientation spread (GOS) less than 2° refer to fully recrystallized grains [ 43 ]. The recrystallization morphologies of N50 steel when deformed at 1000 °C and 1150 °C, at various strain rates, are presented in Fig. 8 . The corresponding changes in recrystallization volume fraction are displayed in Fig. 9 . Under all deformation rate conditions, the global recrystallization at 1150 °C is significantly higher than that at 1000 °C, which is caused by temperature promotion as discussed in Chapter 3.2.1. The effect of strain rate on the global recrystallization at 1000 °C and 1150 °C is similar, namely, when the strain rate ＜1/s, the volume fraction of overall recrystallization gradually decreases with strain rate rising. However, the global recrystallization shows a qualitative leap when the strain rate increases to 10/s and 50/s. 
