There are still some controversy among scholars about the effect of deformation rate on austenitic DRX, with some believing that increasing the strain rate will significantly reduce the kinetics of austenitic DRX, and insisting that the deformation time is too short to nucleate and grow for DRX grains [ [44] , [45] , [46] , [47] , [48] ]. However, some studies have also found [ [49] , [50] , [51] , [52] ] that the effect of strain rate on recrystallization exhibits a threshold, namely, the DRX monotonously decreases at the beginning with increasing deformation rate, but increases significantly after exceeding a certain limit strain rate, which similar to the experimental results of our study. Most studies have explained the facilitating effect from two perspectives: on the one hand, the adiabatic heat becomes significant after exceeding the threshold, and the heat generated cannot quickly dissipate, thereby accelerating the nucleation and growth process of DRX. The adiabatic temperature rise in this work can be estimated by the following equation [ 53 ]: (9) ŋ Δ T = 0.95 ŋ ρ C p ∫ 0 ε σ d ε Where Δ T is the change in temperature, ŋ is the adiabatic correction factor (set as 0.95 when έ ≥ 1/s [ 49 , 54 ]), ∫ 0 ε σ d ε is the area under the stress-train curve, ρ is the density (7.82 g/cm 3 ), C p is the specific heat (0.60 J/g/K at 1150 °C for N50 steel). The adiabatic temperature rises at 10/s and 50/s can reach to 33.0 °C and 41.9 °C respectively, which may have significant effect on global recrystallization. On the other hand, high strain rates will cause dislocations to proliferate rapidly and cannot undergo rearrangement or annihilation in a very short time, thereby the strain energy U stored in the matrix is enhanced quickly. The critical DRX nuclei radius r c related with U can be expressed as [ 49 , 50 ]: (10) r c = 2 γ b U 
 
The critical r c derived from Eq. (10) is smaller because of the higher stored energy U at high strain rates. Therefore, the combined effects of adiabatic heat and higher stored energy during high-strain rate deformation lead to a significant increase of DRX. However, why is the GOS of recrystallized grains at 10/s and 50/s ( Fig. 8 g and (h)) lower than that of 0.01/s( Fig. 8 g)? And the average geometrically necessary dislocation density (ρ GND ) at 10/s (1.55 × 10 14 m −2 as shown in Figs. 8 k) and 50/s (1.49 × 10 14 m −2 as shown in Fig. 8 l) was also smaller than that of 0.01/s (3.33 × 10 14 m −2 as shown Fig. 8 i), which suggests distortionless equiaxed grains when deformation at high strain rate. We believe that some other recrystallization mechanism must have occurred. 
 
It is well known that the DRX grains refer to the grains that nucleate and grow during the deformation process, as a result of more substructures existing within the grains, like grains in Fig. 8 i. While if the material is kept at a relatively high temperature after severe deformation stopped, such as during the gap between two forging steps or during the necessary slow cooling of massive industrial pieces, metadynamics recrystallization (MDRX) and static recrystallization (SRX) can occur and contribute to Post-DRX [ 51 , [55] , [56] , [57] , [58] , [59] ]. The MDRX refers to the rapid growth of the nucleated grains using residual heat and the SRX consists of both nucleation and growth of new grains after deformation stopped. Therefore, the distortionless equiaxed grains obtained at high strain rates ( Fig. 8 g and h) may be caused by Post-DRX. 
 
Most previous works on the effect of strain rate on dynamic recrystallization kinetics were done by quenching samples right after deformation, without discriminating DRX and Post-DRX grains. Those works ignore the influence of quenching delays (including unloading the machine vacuum, taking the sample from the vacuum chamber, and quenching), and thus conclude that increasing strain rate beyond a critical value leads to an increase in dynamic recrystallization kinetics. However, experimental quenching of the majority of previous works besides ours cannot be shorter than a few seconds, which is sufficient to get a significant increase in recrystallized fraction by Post-dynamic mechanisms. A. Nicolaÿ [ 51 ] believed that when the strain rate exceeds 0.1/s, Post-DRX significantly increases with the increase of strain rate, and though reducing the quenching delays less than 2 s, Post-DRX massively occurs and leads to a pronounced increase in the global recrystallized fraction. Therefore, we believe that the enhanced global recrystallization is the result of the combined effect of enhanced DRX and Post-DRX, with the final manifestation of undistorted equiaxed grains being primarily caused by Post-DRX. A. Nicolaÿ [ 51 ] and Koundinya [ 56 ] emphasized that Post-DRX is the main reason for the increase in overall recrystallization. However, the mature DRX grains that have developed in the later stage could also undergo the Post-DRX process to further reduce dislocation density. Therefore, it is difficult to distinguish the contribution of two mechanisms to the overall recrystallization. 
 
An in-situ water cooling experiment with quenching delay less than 2 s was carried out to identify the notable Post-DRX. The built-in cooling water pipe was instantaneously sprayed after deformation at 1150 °C with a strain rate of 50/s. The microstructure characterized by EBSD and ECCI are shown in Fig. 10 (a–c). There are a large number of unrecrystallized regions ( Fig. 10 a), which may be due to the significant inhibition of Post-DRX by in-situ water cooling. Therefore, it can be concluded that the general Post-DRX induced by quenching delay is very important for the significant increase of global recrystallization at high strain rates. The backscattered electron intensity in Post-DRX grains is highly homogeneous, indicating their uniform orientation [ 60 ]. Therefore, it is postulated that the necklace-like recrystallized grains with uniform orientation like grains 1–5 in Fig. 10 c may be instant recrystallized grains by Post-DRX owing to the fact that achieving instantaneous quenching was not technologically possible. Given that a DRX grain cannot exhibit uniform characteristics during the electron channeling process [ 60 ], the recrystallized grains (as indicated by the yellow arrow in Fig. 10 b) with poor contrast still exist dislocation or substructure characteristics of DRX. 
