3.3. Hot process map 
 
The constitutive equation in Section 3.1 can only reflect the relationship between flow stress and variable parameters such as strain rate and temperature. To obtain a reasonable hot working window and avoid flow instability, Prasad and Gegel [ 61 ] proposed a thermal processing map based on the dynamic materials model (DMM) to facilitate the design of thermal processing parameters and the optimization of thermal processing structure. According to the model, energy input P is dissipated in two ways, as depicted in formula (9) : one part (G) is the loss of energy during plastic deformation , and the other part (J) is the consumption of energy due to organizational evolution, such as DRV, DRX, and phase transition, etc. (11) P = σ ⋅ ε ˙ = G + J = ∫ 0 ε ˙ σ ⋅ d ε ˙ + ∫ 0 σ ε ˙ ⋅ d σ 
 
Generally, the flow stress in the simplest form is expressed as [ 61 , 62 ]: (12) σ = K ε ˙ m where σ, έ, and K are flow stress, strain rate, and material constant, respectively, m represents the strain rate sensitivity factor and is regarded to be the distribution coefficient between J and G [ 63 ]: (13) m = d J d G = [ ∂ ( ln σ ) ∂ ( ln ε ˙ ) ] ε ˙ , T 
 
The value of parameter m varies with deformation and strain rate, ranging from 0 to 1. When m equals 1, it indicates that the material is in the ideal linear dissipative state, and the corresponding J value reaches its maximum value, denoted as J max . This maximum value represents the dissipation of half of the total energy due to microstructure evolution. The parameter ŋ reflects the amount of energy consumed by microstructure evolution during deformation and can be expressed as follows: (14) η = J J max = 2 m m + 1 
 
Higher ŋ indicates higher energy consumption for microstructure evolution. However, flow instability, such as wedge cracking and adiabatic shear bands , may also lead to high energy dissipation. To avoid such a situation, Ziegler [ 2 ] proposed an instability factor ξ, which is expressed as follows: (15) ξ = ∂ ln J ∂ ln ε ˙ = ∂ ln ( m m + 1 ) ∂ ln ε ˙ + m < 0 
 
When ξ < 0, there is a possibility of plastic instability. Therefore, the parameter ξ can be combined with the dissipation factor ŋ to further refine the hot processing window. The instability diagrams and energy dissipation diagrams are constructed through the peak stress values ( Table S1 ) obtained from the true stress-true strain curve ( Fig. 2 ). These two diagrams are superimposed to generate the hot processing map for N50 steel as displayed in Fig. 11 . The red region represents ξ < 0, indicating the instability region, while the other regions correspond to non-instability regions. Contour values illustrate the distribution of energy dissipation factors. It is favorable for thermal processing when the dissipation factor exceeds 0.30 [ 64 , 65 ]. Based on Fig. 11 , the recommended heat treatment windows for N50 steel (blue lines in Fig. 11 ) are as follows: a temperature range of 1075–1250 °C and a low strain rate (<0.0183/s), as well as a higher temperature range of 1139–1220 °C accompanied by high strain rate conditions of 31.8–50/s. The microstructure of different positions within the map (I and II in the instability zone, III and IV in the non-instability zone) were observed. 
