3.4. Simulation for the hot extrusion process 
 
Hot extrusion forming is a unique three-dimensional compressive stress process in which the material experiences strong compressive stress in two directions and weak compressive stress in the remaining direction. This leads to material flow in the direction of lower resistance. The schematic diagrams of the hot extrusion and extrusion die are shown in Fig. 2 . To prevent overheating, it is necessary to control the extrusion temperature below the “liquidus” by around 150–200 °C. Moreover, hot extrusion typically involves higher extrusion speeds, primarily because time, or extrusion speed, holds great value in the extrusion industry [ 66 ]. Additionally, the N50 hot extrusion billet directly contacts the extrusion cylinder, indicating that slower hot extrusion rates will cause faster temperature drops and a substantial increase in deformation resistance. It is also important to note that prolonged extrusion times can cause significant wear on the extrusion die, particularly at the outlet. As discussed above, despite the higher dissipation factor (ŋ) observed at low strain rates ( Fig. 11 ), the hot extrusion process was simulated at another possible processing window of 1139–1220 °C, accompanied by high strain rate conditions of 31.8–50/s. Different temperatures (1150 °C, 1180 °C, 1220 °C) with different high extruded rates (50 mm/s, 100 mm/s, 150 mm/s) were adopted in Deform-3D finite elements simulation. The effects of hot extrusion temperature and speed on temperature rise, maximum extrusion force, and forming stability were analyzed to determine the optimal hot extrusion parameters. In the simulation process, the extrusion die and extrusion cylinder were fixed, and the extrusion cylinder and extrusion mandrel were treated as rigid bodies. The constitutive model established in Chapter 3.1 was implanted into the software, and the other thermophysical parameters required for simulation are detailed in Table S2 . The shear friction coefficient was set to 0.03. 
 
Under the extrusion velocity of 50 mm/s (average strain rate of 28.5/s), a significant temperature drop occurs due to the prolonged contact time between the billet and the extrusion cylinder. As depicted in Fig. 13 a, at all extrusion temperatures, the temperature at Point 1 on the billet surface falls below 800 °C, resulting in the solidification of the lubricating glass powder and a significant increase in the deformation resistance of the billet, and finally leading to surface cracking and a decrease in surface quality. During high-speed extrusion at 150 mm/s (average strain rate of 97/s), the strain rate from point P to surface point N at the extrusion exit ( Fig. 13 b) significantly increases to 150/s. Such a high strain rate increases the risk of the adiabatic shear band caused by high strain rate mechanical loads [ [67] , [68] , [69] , [70] ] and also causes a huge temperature rise. As shown in Fig. 13 c, the temperature from point P to point N at the exit surface during extrusion at 1180 °C rises to 1300 °C, indicating the risk of overheating. Based on comprehensive analysis, 100 mm/s (average strain rate of 55/s) is determined as a good choice. Simultaneously, 1180 °C is chosen as the optimal extrusion temperature, as low-temperature extrusion (1150 °C) with higher extrusive force is limited by machine capacity, and high-temperature extrusion (1220 °C) may result in overheating and grain boundary melting. 
