Since the YBCO high-temperature superconducting material possess cryogenic superconducting properties without aging treatment, while the Nb3Sn superconducting wire must undergo this aging process. Therefore, the prepared pipes were subjected to compaction, and compaction & aging treatment to evaluate whether they were suitable for the superconducting jacket of both. 
 
According to the 4.2 K tensile data shown in Table 2 , after compaction, the average yield strength of the corners and surfaces was 1581 ± 60.0 and 1404 ± 130.2 MPa, respectively. The average tensile strength was 1927 ± 22.9 and 1785 ± 118.5 MPa, and the elongation reached an average of 28 ± 4.2 and 29 ± 3.1 %, respectively. Compared to the forging materials in previous studies [ [9] , [10] , [11] ], the strength of the jacket was significantly improved. This is mainly due to the smaller grain size of pipes, which presents an excellent fine grain strengthening effect [ 71 ], additionally, the cold working resulting from the compaction process has also contributed to the overall enhancement of strength. After undergoing compaction combined with aging treatment, the average yield strength of the corners and surfaces can reach 1617 ± 53.2 and 1498 ± 42.8 MPa, respectively. The tensile strength can reach 1907 ± 24.8 and 1879 ± 21.4 MPa, and the elongation can reach 27 ± 4.5 % and 29 ± 3.5 %, respectively. The influence of the 650 °C aging treatment on the strength is not significant. The aging process may have some precipitation-strengthening effect, but it may also cause recovery in compacted pipes, and the two effects may offset each other. According to Table 3 , the fracture toughness of the conduit decreases from 163.3 MPa•m 1/2 in the diameter-reduced state to 145.3 MPa•m 1/2 after aging treatment. Combined with the tensile ductility before and after aging treatment, it can be concluded that the aging process does not significantly affect the ductility of the conduit. This is mainly because the sensitization of modified N50 steel has been suppressed significantly [ 9 , 10 ]. 
 
