The final performance of the conduit material was compared with several states of low-temperature steel ( Fig. 16 ), such as ITER-grade 316LN (cold worked & aging conduit [ [72] , [73] , [74] ], annealed [ 75 , 76 ], only cold worked [ 75 ] and wrought [ 74 ]), JK2LB (as-received [ 77 , 78 ]and cold worked & aging conduit [ 77 , [79] , [80] , [81] , [82] , [83] ], only cold worked [ 79 , 80 ], and only aging [ 78 ] conduit, solution treatment billet [ 78 ] and forgings [ 74 ]), XM-19(annealed forging [ 71 ]), initial N50 (annealed conduit [ 84 ], annealed forgings [ 9 , 11 , [85] , [86] , [87] ]), and high-entropy alloys (cold worked & aged [ 88 ]). The results show that the superconducting coil jacket conduit made from modified N50 exhibits high strength, as well as superior fracture toughness and tensile ductility. It can basically meet the requirements for high strength and toughness of materials under higher magnetic field strengths in the new generation of superconducting coil jacket materials. 
