4. Conclusions 
 
The recrystallization behavior of modified N50 steel during high strain rate processing was studied through isothermal compression experiments, and the forming parameters of superconducting coil jacket pipes were formulated by processing map and finite element simulation. The conduit was fabricated finally and its performance was evaluated. The main conclusions obtained are as follows: 
 
(1) The thermal activation energy Q of modified N50 steel is 578706.9 kJ/mol, significantly higher than that of ordinary austenitic steel . The constitutive equation is: ε ˙ = 2.92 × 10 21 [ sinh ( 0.0048679 σ ) ] 6.832 exp ( − 578706.9 R T ) 
 
    (2) 
 
    The DDRX is the dominant mechanism of N50 steel at all temperatures. There is a decrease in global recrystallization with increasing strain rate, up to a threshold 1/s beyond which the global recrystallization began significantly promotion owing to not only the adiabatic heat and higher stored energy, but also quick Post-DRX mechanism induced by delay quenching. 
    (3) 
 
    The optimal hot extrusion temperature was determined to be 1180 °C, with an extrusion velocity of 100 mm/s based on the infinite simulation and hot deformation map. 
    (4) 
 
    N50 superconducting coil pipes with Post-DRX features were successfully fabricated by hot extrusion. The 4.2 K strength of the final jacket was far beyond traditional cryogenic steels: YS of 1312–1674 MPa; UTS of 1698∼1945 MPa; and excellent 4.2 K ductile and fracture toughness were also obtained: EL of 21.5–32.5 %; K IC :137∼171 MPa m 1/2 . 
 
